      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as he does with the two 20-pound weights, we first need to calculate the total weight he lifts with the two 20-pound weights.\n\nThe weight of each set of weights is:\n\\[ 20 \\text{ pounds} \\times 12 \\text{ times} = 240 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nSo, the total weight Terrell lifts with two 20-pound weights is 240 pounds. We need to find out how many times the two 15-pound weights must be lifted so that the total weight is also 240 pounds.\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights. The total weight lifted is:\n\\[ 15 \\text{ pounds} \\times x \\text{ times} = 15x \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nWe set this equal to the total weight lifted with the two 20-pound weights:\n\\[ 15x = 240 \\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve the equation by dividing both sides by 15:\n\\[ x = \\frac{240}{15} = 16 \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight as he does with the two 20-pound weights.",
