      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as the original weights, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight Terrell lifts when he uses two 20-pound weights.\n2. Determine the total weight Terrell lifts when he uses two 15-pound weights.\n3. Set the total weight lifted in the new scenario equal to the total weight from the original scenario and solve for the number of lifts.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights.**\n\nTerrell lifts two 20-pound weights, and he lifts them 12 times.\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight} = 2 \\times 20 \\times 12 = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the total weight lifted with two 15-pound weights.**\n\nLet \\( n \\) be the number of times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight.\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight} = 2 \\times 15 \\times n = 30n \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Set the total weight lifted in the new scenario equal to the total weight from the original scenario and solve for \\( n \\).**\n\nWe know the total weight lifted in the new scenario must be equal to 480 pounds.\n\\[\n30n = 480\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( n \\), divide both sides of the equation by 30:\n\\[\nn = \\frac{480}{30} = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
